window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["ml-algorithms"]=[{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0001","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, why is the feature matrix called <code>X</code> (capital) and the label <code>y</code> (lowercase), and what shape does each need?","a":"<p><code>X</code> is a 2D matrix with one row per sample and one column per feature. <code>y</code> is a 1D vector with one label per sample. With pandas you pass <code>df[['col']]</code> for X (a DataFrame, 2D) and <code>df['col']</code> for y (a Series, 1D). Passing a 1D Series as X raises an error asking you to reshape, because scikit-learn cannot tell whether it is one sample or one feature.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0002","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, what does a trailing underscore on an attribute such as <code>coef_</code>, <code>intercept_</code> or <code>cluster_centers_</code> tell you?","a":"<p>The value is learned from data, so it exists only after <code>.fit()</code> has run. Reading it on an unfitted estimator raises <code>NotFittedError</code> or <code>AttributeError</code>. Constructor arguments (hyperparameters such as <code>n_clusters</code>) have no underscore and can be read at any time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0003","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"Why is it useful that every scikit-learn estimator shares the same <code>fit</code> / <code>predict</code> / <code>score</code> interface?","a":"<p>You can swap one model class for another and leave the rest of the code unchanged. This makes it cheap to try a baseline, then a stronger model, on the same data. It also lets tools such as <code>Pipeline</code>, <code>GridSearchCV</code> and <code>cross_val_score</code> work with any estimator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0004","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, when do you call <code>.fit(X)</code> and when <code>.fit(X, y)</code>?","a":"<p>Call <code>.fit(X)</code> for unsupervised models (clustering, PCA), which learn structure from features alone. Call <code>.fit(X, y)</code> for supervised models (regression, classification), which learn a mapping from features to labels. Transformers such as <code>StandardScaler</code> also use <code>.fit(X)</code> because they only learn statistics of the features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0005","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, what does <code>.score(X, y)</code> return for a regressor versus a classifier?","a":"<p>A regressor returns R², the share of target variance explained (1.0 is perfect, and it can be negative on unseen data if the model is worse than predicting the mean). A classifier returns accuracy, the fraction of correct predictions. Both are only meaningful if you pass data the model did not train on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0006","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, what is the difference between <code>.transform(X)</code> and <code>.fit_transform(X)</code>, and which one do you call on test data?","a":"<p><code>.fit_transform(X)</code> learns the transformer's statistics from X and applies them in one call. <code>.transform(X)</code> applies statistics that were already learned. Use <code>.fit_transform</code> on training data and only <code>.transform</code> on validation or test data. Calling fit on the test set leaks test information into preprocessing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0007","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn 1.2 and later, what does <code>set_config(transform_output='pandas')</code> do, and why is it handy?","a":"<p>It makes transformers such as <code>StandardScaler</code> and <code>PCA</code> return pandas DataFrames instead of NumPy arrays. Column names and the row index are preserved, so you can keep using <code>.describe()</code>, label-based column access and index alignment with the original data. Earlier versions return NumPy arrays only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0008","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"How does the K-means algorithm work, step by step?","a":"<p>Choose k, then pick k initial centroids. Assign every point to its nearest centroid. Move each centroid to the mean of the points assigned to it. Repeat the assign and move steps until assignments stop changing or the iteration limit is reached. It is guaranteed to converge, but only to a local optimum.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0009","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"Why must you scale features before running K-means?","a":"<p>K-means uses Euclidean distance. A feature with a large numeric range, such as income in dollars next to age in years, dominates the distance and the other features barely matter. Standardizing each feature to mean 0 and standard deviation 1 gives every feature a fair say.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0010","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"In scikit-learn's <code>KMeans</code>, what does <code>n_init</code> control and why does it matter?","a":"<p>K-means depends on its starting centroids and can land in a poor local optimum. <code>n_init</code> is the number of times the algorithm runs with different starting centroids, and the run with the lowest inertia is kept. A larger value costs time but gives more stable results. <code>n_init='auto'</code> is accepted from scikit-learn 1.2 and is the default from 1.4. Older versions defaulted to 10.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0011","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"In scikit-learn's <code>KMeans</code>, what is the difference between <code>init='random'</code> and the default <code>init='k-means++'</code>?","a":"<p><code>'random'</code> picks k random data points as starting centroids. <code>'k-means++'</code> picks the first at random and then favours points far from the ones already chosen, which spreads the centroids out. It usually converges faster and to a better solution, so it is the default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0012","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"In scikit-learn's <code>KMeans</code>, what does <code>max_iter</code> limit, and what do you expect from a run with <code>max_iter=1</code> and <code>n_init=1</code>?","a":"<p><code>max_iter</code> caps the number of assign-and-update rounds in a single run. With a value of 1 the centroids move only once from their starting positions, so the clusters are rough and unconverged. Raising it step by step is a good way to visualize how centroids walk toward their final positions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0013","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"What are the main weaknesses of K-means, and which alternative addresses each?","a":"<p>It needs k up front, assumes roughly round clusters of similar size, is sensitive to outliers because the mean is pulled by them, and depends on initialization. Hierarchical clustering removes the need to pick k in advance, DBSCAN handles arbitrary shapes and outliers, and a Gaussian mixture handles elliptical clusters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0014","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"After fitting a scikit-learn <code>KMeans</code> model, what does <code>.predict(X_new)</code> do for a point it has never seen?","a":"<p>It assigns the point to the nearest learned centroid and returns that cluster index. It does not refit or move any centroid. <code>cluster_centers_</code> holds the centroid coordinates and <code>labels_</code> holds the assignments of the training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0015","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"What is inertia in K-means, and why can you not just pick the k with the lowest inertia?","a":"<p>Inertia is the sum of squared distances from each point to its nearest centroid. It falls every time k grows, and reaches zero when k equals the number of points, which is useless. So you look for the point where adding clusters stops paying off, not for the minimum.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0016","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How do you use the elbow method to choose k?","a":"<p>Fit K-means for a range of k values, plot inertia against k, and pick the bend where the curve flattens, because after that each extra cluster gains little. If several k values look plausible, prefer the smaller one. When the curve is smooth with no clear bend, the elbow method is inconclusive and you need another signal such as silhouette.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0017","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"In this scikit-learn loop, what is <code>inertia_</code> and when is it available?","a":"<p><code>inertia_</code> is an attribute of a fitted <code>KMeans</code> object holding the within-cluster sum of squares for that run. It exists only after <code>fit</code>. The loop collects one value per k so you can plot the elbow curve. <code>random_state=42</code> makes each fit repeatable so the curve does not wobble between runs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inertias = []\nfor i in range(20):\n    km = KMeans(n_clusters=i+1, n_init=&#x27;auto&#x27;, random_state=42)\n    km.fit(X)\n    inertias.append(km.inertia_)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0018","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How would you justify a chosen k to a stakeholder when metrics are ambiguous?","a":"<p>Combine the metrics with domain meaning. Show the elbow and silhouette results, and when several k values score about the same, take the smaller one because it is simpler to explain and act on. Then describe each cluster in business terms to show the segments are distinct and useful. Choosing k is a judgment call, not a formula.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0019","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"What does the silhouette coefficient measure, and what range does it have?","a":"<p>For each point it compares the average distance to its own cluster (a) with the average distance to the nearest other cluster (b): (b - a) / max(a, b). Values run from -1 to 1. Near 1 means the point sits well inside its cluster, near 0 means it is on a boundary, and negative means it is probably in the wrong cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0020","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How do you read a silhouette plot for K-means?","a":"<p>Each cluster appears as a blade, with one bar per point sorted by score, and a dashed line at the average score. A good clustering has blades that are wide and bulging, all reaching past the average line, with similar thickness. Warning signs are thin pointy blades, a cluster that stays under the average, and bars with negative scores.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0021","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How do the elbow method and the silhouette score differ, and what do you do when they disagree?","a":"<p>The elbow looks only at compactness (inertia). Silhouette also measures separation between clusters, and gives a per-point and per-cluster view. When they disagree, treat each as evidence, prefer the simpler k if scores are close, and use cluster interpretability and business needs to break the tie.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0022","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"Why do people compute the silhouette on a random sample of a large dataset?","a":"<p>Silhouette needs pairwise distances, so its cost grows roughly with the square of the number of points. A sample of a few thousand rows gives nearly the same picture in a fraction of the time. Fix <code>random_state</code> when sampling so the plot is repeatable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0023","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"In the Yellowbrick library, what does <code>silhouette_visualizer</code> do when you pass it an unfitted <code>KMeans</code> model and data?","a":"<p>It fits the model on the data and draws the silhouette plot, showing per-cluster blades and the average score, in one call. It saves you from calling <code>fit</code>, computing <code>silhouette_samples</code> and plotting by hand. A comparable number without a plot is available from <code>sklearn.metrics.silhouette_score</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0024","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"Why is interpreting clusters at least as important as producing them?","a":"<p>Clustering is unsupervised, so the algorithm returns numbers with no meaning. A cluster only has value if you can say what it represents, for example a high-spend, low-frequency segment, and act on it. Without interpretation you cannot validate that the clusters are sensible or useful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0025","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"How do you describe clusters by group means in pandas, and why transpose the result?","a":"<p><code>assign</code> adds the cluster label as a column, <code>groupby('cluster')</code> plus <code>agg('mean')</code> gives the average of each feature per cluster. <code>.T</code> puts clusters in columns and features in rows, which reads better when there are many features. <code>background_gradient(..., axis='columns')</code> colors each row relative to its own range, so you can see which cluster is high or low for each feature.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>(df\n .assign(cluster=label)\n .groupby(&#x27;cluster&#x27;)\n .agg(&#x27;mean&#x27;, numeric_only=True)\n .T\n .style.background_gradient(cmap=&#x27;RdBu&#x27;, axis=&#x27;columns&#x27;)\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0026","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"What is a surrogate decision tree for explaining clusters, and how do you build one?","a":"<p>Train a shallow <code>DecisionTreeClassifier</code> with the features as X and the K-means cluster labels as y. Then read the tree: each split shows which feature and threshold separates groups, and the leaves are clusters. Keep <code>max_depth</code> small so the tree stays readable. Its feature importances also show which inputs drive the segmentation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0027","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"What is the limitation of explaining clusters with a surrogate tree?","a":"<p>The tree only approximates the clustering. If its accuracy at predicting the labels is low, its rules do not describe the clusters faithfully. Check the tree's accuracy against the cluster labels before trusting the explanation. The tree also explains the labels, not why K-means chose them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0028","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"How can PCA help you inspect K-means clusters visually?","a":"<p>Reduce the features to two or three principal components, scatter-plot them, and color the points by cluster label. You can see how separated the clusters are and whether any overlap. Then look at the PCA component weights to see which original features drive the separation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0029","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"How does agglomerative hierarchical clustering work, and what is a dendrogram?","a":"<p>Start with every point as its own cluster, then repeatedly merge the two closest clusters until one remains, recording the distance at each merge. A dendrogram is the tree of those merges with merge distance on the vertical axis. Cutting it at a chosen height gives a number of clusters, so you pick k after seeing the structure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0030","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"In hierarchical clustering, what is the linkage setting and why does it change the result?","a":"<p>Linkage defines the distance between two clusters when merging. Single uses the closest pair of points and can chain clusters together. Complete uses the farthest pair and favors compact clusters. Average uses the mean pair distance. Ward merges the pair that least increases within-cluster variance and tends to give balanced, K-means-like clusters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0031","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"Why does hierarchical clustering scale poorly?","a":"<p>It needs distances between all pairs of points, so memory and time grow at least quadratically with the number of points. It is practical for thousands of rows, not millions. K-means scales roughly linearly, which is why it is the default for big data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0032","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"How does DBSCAN form clusters?","a":"<p>A point with at least <code>min_samples</code> neighbors within distance <code>eps</code> is a core point. Clusters grow by chaining core points that are within <code>eps</code> of each other, and non-core points within reach of a core point join as border points. Points that are in no cluster are labeled noise (label -1 in scikit-learn).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0033","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"What do <code>eps</code> and <code>min_samples</code> control in scikit-learn's <code>DBSCAN</code>, and what goes wrong with bad values?","a":"<p><code>eps</code> is the neighborhood radius and <code>min_samples</code> is the number of points needed to call a region dense. If <code>eps</code> is too small, most points become noise. If it is too large, separate clusters merge into one. A k-distance plot is a common way to choose <code>eps</code>. Features should be scaled first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0034","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"When would you choose DBSCAN over K-means?","a":"<p>When clusters are non-round (rings, crescents), when you do not know how many clusters exist, or when you must flag outliers. It struggles when clusters have very different densities, since a single <code>eps</code> cannot fit them all, and in very high dimensions where distances lose meaning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0035","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"Why can you call <code>.predict</code> on a fitted <code>KMeans</code> but not on <code>DBSCAN</code> or <code>AgglomerativeClustering</code> in scikit-learn?","a":"<p>K-means keeps centroids, so a new point can be assigned to the nearest one. DBSCAN and agglomerative clustering have no such summary, so they only label the data you give them. Use <code>fit_predict(X)</code> to get labels, or train a classifier on the labels if you need to score new points.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0036","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"Why select the columns you want with <code>.loc[:, [...]]</code> instead of dropping the ones you do not want?","a":"<p>An explicit list of kept columns is clearer and safer. If the source data gains a new column, a drop list lets it slip into the model, while a keep list does not. The code also documents which features the model uses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0037","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"Why must non-numeric columns be removed or encoded before running K-means, and what is the encoding trade-off?","a":"<p>K-means computes Euclidean distances and means, which are undefined for strings. One-hot encoding turns categories into 0/1 columns, but then means and distances on those columns are only a rough fit, and many one-hot columns can dominate the distance. K-modes or K-prototypes are alternatives made for categorical data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0038","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"What are the options for missing values before clustering, and what does <code>dropna()</code> cost you?","a":"<p>You can drop rows, drop columns, or impute (for example with the median or <code>SimpleImputer</code>). <code>dropna()</code> is simple but removes whole rows, which can shrink the data a lot and bias it if the missingness is not random. When many rows would be lost, imputing is usually better.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0039","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"What does <code>StandardScaler</code> do mathematically, and how does it compare with <code>MinMaxScaler</code> and <code>RobustScaler</code>?","a":"<p><code>StandardScaler</code> computes (x - mean) / std for each column, giving mean 0 and standard deviation 1. <code>MinMaxScaler</code> rescales to a fixed range, usually 0 to 1, and is sensitive to outliers. <code>RobustScaler</code> uses median and interquartile range, so outliers affect it far less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0040","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"What does PCA do, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>PCA finds new axes (principal components), each a linear combination of the original features, ordered so the first captures the most variance, the second the most remaining variance, and so on. You keep only the first few components. This reduces the number of columns, lets you visualize high-dimensional data in 2D or 3D, and removes redundancy between correlated features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0041","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"Why should you standardize features before PCA?","a":"<p>PCA chases variance, and variance depends on units. A feature measured in large numbers has a huge variance and takes over the first component even if it carries no more information. Standardizing gives every feature variance 1 so the components reflect correlation structure instead of scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0042","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"What are the limitations of PCA?","a":"<p>It only captures linear relationships, so curved structure is missed. It is unsupervised, so the highest-variance directions are not necessarily the ones that predict your target. Components are mixtures of features, which makes them harder to explain than raw columns. It is also sensitive to outliers and to scaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0043","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"How does PCA differ from feature selection?","a":"<p>Feature selection keeps a subset of the original columns and drops the rest. PCA builds new columns as weighted combinations of all of them. Selection keeps interpretability, PCA usually compresses information better but the new features are less readable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0044","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"List practical uses of PCA in a machine learning workflow.","a":"<p>Visualizing high-dimensional data and clusters in 2D or 3D, speeding up training by reducing columns, reducing multicollinearity among features, denoising by dropping low-variance components, and discovering which original features carry most of the variance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0045","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"In scikit-learn, what is the difference between <code>PCA(n_components=2)</code> and <code>PCA(n_components=0.95)</code>?","a":"<p>An integer keeps exactly that many components. A float between 0 and 1 keeps the smallest number of components whose cumulative explained variance reaches that fraction, here 95 percent. The float form is convenient when you want to compress without guessing how many components are needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0046","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"What happens when you create <code>PCA()</code> without <code>n_components</code>?","a":"<p>It keeps all components, up to the smaller of the number of samples and number of features. Use it to inspect the full explained-variance spectrum, then refit with fewer components or look at the cumulative sum to decide how many to keep.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0047","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"In scikit-learn, what is the order of calls to run PCA and get the reduced data, and what does each do?","a":"<p><code>PCA(n_components=...)</code> sets the hyperparameter. <code>.fit(X)</code> learns the components and the variance statistics. <code>.transform(X)</code> projects the data onto them and returns the principal component scores. <code>.fit_transform(X)</code> does both in one call. Apply the same fitted PCA to new data with <code>.transform</code> only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0048","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"When you color a PCA scatter plot by the class label, but PCA never saw the labels, what does separation tell you?","a":"<p>It shows the classes differ in the directions of greatest variance, so the features carry class information and a simple model is likely to work. It does not prove separability in general. Lack of separation can mean the structure is non-linear or lives in lower-variance directions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0049","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"In scikit-learn with <code>set_config(transform_output='pandas')</code>, how are PCA output columns named and how do you rename them to PC1, PC2?","a":"<p>scikit-learn names them <code>pca0</code>, <code>pca1</code>, and so on. Rename them with <code>df.rename(columns=...)</code> using a function or dictionary, for example mapping <code>pca0</code> to <code>PC1</code>. The names are only labels, the numbers are unchanged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0050","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"What are the three main outputs of a fitted PCA and what does each tell you?","a":"<p>The transformed data (principal component scores, same rows as the input), <code>explained_variance_ratio_</code> (share of total variance each component holds, in descending order), and <code>components_</code> (the weight of each original feature in each component).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0051","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How do you decide how many principal components to keep?","a":"<p>Take the cumulative sum of <code>explained_variance_ratio_</code> and keep the smallest number reaching a target such as 95 percent. A scree plot of the ratios, where you look for the bend where the curve flattens, is a second guide. Choose by how much information you can afford to lose and how much speedup you need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0052","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"What does a scree plot show and how is it read?","a":"<p>It plots explained variance (y) for each component number (x). The first components stand tall and later ones taper off. The bend after which the bars are small suggests how many components matter, and the rest mostly carry noise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0053","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"In scikit-learn, what shape does <code>pca.components_</code> have and how do you interpret a row?","a":"<p>Shape is (n_components, n_features). Each row is one principal component and each entry is the weight of one original feature in it. A large absolute weight means that feature strongly shapes the component. A value near 0 means it barely matters. Sign shows direction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0054","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"Can the <code>components_</code> weights be used as feature importance?","a":"<p>Only when features share a scale, so standardize first. Even then they describe importance for explaining variance, not for predicting a target. A feature with large variance and no predictive value can still dominate the first component.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0055","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How is one principal component score computed for a sample?","a":"<p>Center each feature by subtracting its training mean, multiply by that component's weights, and sum. In matrix form it is <code>(X - pca.mean_) @ pca.components_.T</code>. scikit-learn centers for you inside <code>transform</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0056","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"What does it mean that principal components are orthogonal?","a":"<p>Each component is perpendicular to the others, so their scores are uncorrelated. Each component adds new information instead of repeating an earlier one. This is why PCA is used to remove multicollinearity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0057","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How would you implement PCA by hand with NumPy?","a":"<p>Center the data, compute the covariance matrix, take its eigenvalues and eigenvectors, sort them by eigenvalue descending, and project the centered data onto the sorted eigenvectors. Eigenvalues are the variance per component, eigenvectors are the weights.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0058","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"When computing PCA by hand, why is <code>np.linalg.eigh</code> preferred over <code>np.linalg.eig</code> for a covariance matrix?","a":"<p>A covariance matrix is symmetric, so <code>eigh</code> is faster, guarantees real eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenvectors, and returns eigenvalues in ascending order. <code>eig</code> is for general matrices and can return complex values from rounding. In both cases you must sort descending to put the largest-variance component first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0059","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"What numerical method does scikit-learn's <code>PCA</code> use instead of an eigen-decomposition of the covariance matrix?","a":"<p>Singular value decomposition (SVD) of the centered data. It is more numerically stable and avoids forming the covariance matrix explicitly. Component signs are arbitrary, so the same component can flip sign between implementations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0060","card":"q-ml-algorithms-012-4-3-3d-scatter-with-plotly","sec":"ml-algorithms-s011","q":"Why use Plotly's <code>px.scatter_3d</code> instead of a static Matplotlib scatter to look at PCA output?","a":"<p>A Plotly figure is interactive: you rotate, zoom and hover to read values. In 3D a fixed viewing angle often hides separation or overlap, so rotating matters. Static plots remain better for reports and papers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0061","card":"q-ml-algorithms-012-4-3-3d-scatter-with-plotly","sec":"ml-algorithms-s011","q":"In Plotly Express <code>scatter_3d</code>, what does the <code>color</code> argument do when you pass the class label?","a":"<p>It colors each point by the value, so you can check whether classes occupy different regions of the first three components. Use categorical labels for discrete colors. A continuous column gives a color scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0062","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"How do PCA, t-SNE and UMAP differ, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>PCA is linear, keeps global variance, is fast, deterministic and gives interpretable weights, so it suits preprocessing and quick looks. t-SNE preserves local neighborhoods in a non-linear way and is mainly for visualization. UMAP also keeps local structure, usually keeps more global structure, is faster than t-SNE on large data, and can be used on new data. Pick PCA first, then try t-SNE or UMAP when clusters are non-linear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0063","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"What mistakes do people make when reading a t-SNE plot?","a":"<p>They read cluster size, distance between clusters, or relative position as meaningful. t-SNE preserves only local neighborhoods, so those are not reliable. The plot also changes with <code>perplexity</code> and with the random seed. Run it several times and with different perplexities before drawing conclusions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0064","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"In scikit-learn's <code>TSNE</code>, what does <code>perplexity</code> mean and why does <code>random_state</code> matter?","a":"<p>Perplexity is roughly the effective number of neighbors each point considers, so low values stress very local structure and high values broader structure. The optimization starts from a random layout, so without a fixed <code>random_state</code> each run gives a different picture.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0065","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"Why can scikit-learn's <code>TSNE</code> not be used to transform new data after fitting?","a":"<p>It has <code>fit_transform</code> but no <code>transform</code> method. The embedding is an optimization over the specific points given, so there is no learned mapping for new points. UMAP and PCA do provide <code>transform</code>, which makes them usable in production pipelines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0066","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"How do you use UMAP in Python, and what do its main settings do?","a":"<p>Install <code>umap-learn</code> (it is not part of scikit-learn) and use <code>umap.UMAP(n_components=2)</code>, then <code>fit_transform(X)</code>. <code>n_neighbors</code> trades local detail against global structure, and <code>min_dist</code> controls how tightly points are packed. Set <code>random_state</code> for repeatable output, which can limit parallelism.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0067","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"What is a common way to combine PCA with t-SNE on wide data?","a":"<p>Reduce to around 30 to 50 dimensions with PCA first, then run t-SNE. This removes noise and speeds up t-SNE's neighbor computation, which is slow on many features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0068","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"Why drop the target column from the features before running PCA for a classification dataset?","a":"<p>PCA would then include the label in the components, which is target leakage, and coloring by that label would look better than the features deserve. Keep the target aside and use it only for coloring or evaluation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0069","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"After <code>dropna()</code> removes some rows before PCA, how do you color the PCA plot by a label column from the original DataFrame?","a":"<p>Align on the index. With <code>set_config(transform_output='pandas')</code> the PCA output keeps the row index of its input, so <code>raw.loc[X_pca.index, 'label']</code> returns the labels for exactly the kept rows. Passing the full original column fails or misaligns because the lengths differ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0070","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"A PCA scatter colored by outcome shows heavily overlapping classes. What can you conclude and what next?","a":"<p>The first components do not separate the classes linearly, which suggests non-linear relationships or information in other components. Check <code>explained_variance_ratio_</code> and <code>components_</code>, color by other variables, try a non-linear method such as UMAP, and compare against a model such as a tree ensemble.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0071","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"What does linear regression do and what does it minimize?","a":"<p>It fits weights (slopes) and an intercept so that the prediction <code>y = b0 + b1*x1 + ... + bn*xn</code> is as close as possible to the targets. Ordinary least squares chooses the weights that minimize the sum of squared errors between predictions and actual values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0072","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"How do you interpret a coefficient in a linear regression?","a":"<p>It is the expected change in the target for a one-unit increase in that feature while the other features stay fixed. The sign gives the direction, and the magnitude gives the size in the feature's units. The intercept is the prediction when all features are zero.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0073","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"When is linear regression a good choice, and what is its role in practice?","a":"<p>Use it when the relationship is roughly linear, when you need an explainable model, or as a baseline. A fast, simple baseline tells you how much a more complex model such as gradient boosting must improve to be worth its cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0074","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"What are the usual downsides of plain linear regression?","a":"<p>It misses non-linear patterns and interactions unless you add features, is hurt by outliers because errors are squared, becomes unstable with correlated features, and extrapolates a straight line beyond the training range, which can be wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0075","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"How can you fit a linear regression other than gradient descent, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>Ordinary least squares has a closed-form solution (the normal equation), which scikit-learn solves with a stable matrix factorization. It is exact and needs no learning rate, but becomes expensive with a very large number of features. Gradient descent scales better to huge data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0076","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"Which regularized variants of linear regression exist and why use them?","a":"<p>Ridge adds an L2 penalty that shrinks weights and stabilizes correlated features. Lasso adds an L1 penalty that can drive weights to exactly zero, so it also selects features. Elastic net mixes both. They reduce overfitting, and they need standardized inputs because the penalty depends on feature scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0077","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"How do you compute the slope and intercept of a one-feature linear regression by hand?","a":"<p>Slope = sum((x - mean_x) * (y - mean_y)) / sum((x - mean_x)^2), which is covariance of x and y divided by variance of x. Intercept = mean_y - slope * mean_x. Because of that formula the fitted line always passes through the point (mean_x, mean_y).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0078","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"In pandas, what does this expression compute?","a":"<p>It is the least-squares slope for <code>x1</code> and <code>y1</code>, both pandas Series. The numerator sums the products of deviations from the means, the denominator sums the squared deviations of <code>x1</code>. The result is a single float.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>slope = ((x1 - x1.mean()) * (y1 - y1.mean())).sum() / ((x1 - x1.mean())**2).sum()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0079","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"How does the by-hand slope formula extend to many features?","a":"<p>With several features there is one weight per column, solved together with matrix algebra: weights = (X^T X)^-1 X^T y, after adding a column of ones for the intercept. Each weight is the effect of its feature with the others held fixed. Libraries use a stable solver instead of an explicit inverse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0080","card":"q-ml-algorithms-017-5-2-with-scikit-learn","sec":"ml-algorithms-s016","q":"In scikit-learn, why does <code>LinearRegression().fit(df[['x']], df['y'])</code> use double brackets for X and single brackets for y?","a":"<p>X must be 2D (rows by features), and <code>df[['x']]</code> returns a DataFrame while <code>df['x']</code> returns a 1D Series. The target y is expected as a 1D vector. Passing a 1D X raises \"Expected 2D array, got 1D array instead\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0081","card":"q-ml-algorithms-017-5-2-with-scikit-learn","sec":"ml-algorithms-s016","q":"In scikit-learn's <code>LinearRegression</code>, how do you read the learned slope and intercept for one feature?","a":"<p>After <code>fit</code>, <code>coef_</code> is an array with one weight per feature (shape <code>(n_features,)</code>, so <code>coef_[0]</code> for a single feature) and <code>intercept_</code> is a single float. Both end in an underscore because they exist only after fitting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0082","card":"q-ml-algorithms-017-5-2-with-scikit-learn","sec":"ml-algorithms-s016","q":"What does <code>fit_intercept=False</code> change in scikit-learn's <code>LinearRegression</code>?","a":"<p>The model is forced through the origin, with no <code>b0</code>. That is right only if the target really is zero when all features are zero, or when you have centered the data yourself. Otherwise the fit is biased.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0083","card":"q-ml-algorithms-018-5-3-why-visualization-matters-anscombe","sec":"ml-algorithms-s017","q":"What point does Anscombe's quartet make about summary statistics?","a":"<p>Four datasets can share the same means, variances, correlation and fitted line, yet look completely different: one linear, one curved, one with an outlier, one driven by a single leverage point. Statistics alone can hide this, so always plot the data and the residuals.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0084","card":"q-ml-algorithms-018-5-3-why-visualization-matters-anscombe","sec":"ml-algorithms-s017","q":"If a scatter plot shows a curve, what are your options with linear regression?","a":"<p>Add non-linear features such as squared terms or interaction terms, transform the target or features (for example log), or use a model that learns non-linearity such as a tree ensemble. Check a residual plot: a clear pattern means the linear form is wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0085","card":"q-ml-algorithms-018-5-3-why-visualization-matters-anscombe","sec":"ml-algorithms-s017","q":"One outlier is pulling a regression line away from the other points. What do you check and do?","a":"<p>First check whether it is a data error. If it is a real, rare value, decide whether the model should serve it. Options are removing it with justification, using a robust regressor (Huber, RANSAC) or a model less sensitive to outliers, and reporting results with and without it. Squared error makes least squares very sensitive to such points.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0086","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"What do R², MAE and MSE measure, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>R² is the fraction of target variance the model explains (higher is better). MAE is the average absolute error, in the target's units, and is easy to explain. MSE is the average squared error, which punishes large errors more and is in squared units, so people often report its square root (RMSE).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0087","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"When would you prefer MAE over MSE as the metric?","a":"<p>When outliers should not dominate the score, or when the business cost grows linearly with error. Prefer MSE or RMSE when large misses are disproportionately costly, because squaring weights them heavily.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0088","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How can you view which features matter most in a fitted <code>LinearRegression</code>, and what must hold for that to be valid?","a":"<p>Plot <code>coef_</code> as a bar chart indexed by feature names. Larger absolute value means more impact and the sign gives direction. It is only comparable across features if they are on the same scale, so standardize first, and correlated features can split weight between them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0089","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"Why does <code>model.predict(X.iloc[[0]])</code> use double brackets to predict the first row?","a":"<p><code>iloc[[0]]</code> returns a one-row DataFrame (2D), which is what <code>predict</code> requires. <code>iloc[0]</code> returns a 1D Series and causes a shape error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0090","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How do you decide whether an R² score is good enough?","a":"<p>There is no universal cutoff. Compare against a baseline and other models on the same held-out data, then judge against the business value of the prediction. In a noisy domain such as human behavior a modest R² can pay off, while a safety-critical use may need far more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0091","card":"q-ml-algorithms-020-5-5-standardization-same-score-comparabl","sec":"ml-algorithms-s019","q":"Why does standardizing features leave a linear regression's R² unchanged but change its coefficients?","a":"<p>Scaling is a linear change of each feature, so the same fitted predictions are still reachable, and plain least squares gives identical predictions. The coefficients are expressed per unit of each feature, so rescaling changes them. After standardizing, each coefficient is the effect of a one-standard-deviation change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0092","card":"q-ml-algorithms-020-5-5-standardization-same-score-comparabl","sec":"ml-algorithms-s019","q":"When should you standardize for a linear regression?","a":"<p>When you want to compare coefficients across features, and when you use regularization (ridge or lasso), because the penalty treats all weights equally and is scale-dependent. For plain least squares and pure prediction, scaling is not required.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0093","card":"q-ml-algorithms-020-5-5-standardization-same-score-comparabl","sec":"ml-algorithms-s019","q":"A raw-scale coefficient chart ranks a feature as most important, but after standardizing it drops down. What happened?","a":"<p>The feature has small numeric values, so it needed a large coefficient to have an effect, or the reverse. Raw coefficients reflect units, not importance. Standardized coefficients put every feature in standard-deviation units, which gives a fair comparison.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0094","card":"q-ml-algorithms-021-5-6-compare-with-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s020","q":"Why can XGBoost score higher than linear regression on a tabular regression problem?","a":"<p>Boosted trees capture non-linear relationships and feature interactions with no manual feature design, while linear regression only fits a weighted sum. The cost is less interpretability, more hyperparameters and more risk of overfitting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0095","card":"q-ml-algorithms-021-5-6-compare-with-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s020","q":"When would you still prefer linear regression over XGBoost?","a":"<p>When you need clear coefficients for explanation or compliance, when data is small, when the relationship is truly near-linear, when you want fast training and prediction with tiny models, or when extrapolating beyond the training range matters, since trees cannot extrapolate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0096","card":"q-ml-algorithms-021-5-6-compare-with-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s020","q":"What does it show that <code>XGBRegressor</code> supports the same <code>fit</code>, <code>predict</code> and <code>score</code> calls as <code>LinearRegression</code>?","a":"<p>XGBoost ships a scikit-learn-compatible wrapper, so it can slot into pipelines, <code>cross_val_score</code> and <code>GridSearchCV</code> without changes. You can benchmark both models with the same evaluation code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0097","card":"q-ml-algorithms-022-5-7-assumptions","sec":"ml-algorithms-s021","q":"What assumptions does linear regression make and what happens when they break?","a":"<p>A linear relationship between features and target, independent errors, constant error variance (homoscedasticity), no strong multicollinearity, and no extreme outliers; for confidence intervals and p-values, roughly normal errors. Violations give biased fits, unstable or hard-to-interpret coefficients, and unreliable intervals, though predictions may still be useful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0098","card":"q-ml-algorithms-022-5-7-assumptions","sec":"ml-algorithms-s021","q":"What is multicollinearity, how do you detect it, and what do you do?","a":"<p>It is when features are strongly correlated, so the model cannot tell which one deserves the credit, and coefficients become unstable and can flip sign. Detect it with a correlation matrix or variance inflation factors (VIF, a value over about 5 to 10 is a warning). Fix by dropping or combining features, using PCA, or using ridge regression.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0099","card":"q-ml-algorithms-022-5-7-assumptions","sec":"ml-algorithms-s021","q":"What is homoscedasticity and how do you check it?","a":"<p>It means the spread of errors stays constant across the range of predictions. Plot residuals against predicted values: a funnel shape means variance changes (heteroscedasticity). Common remedies are transforming the target (for example log), adding missing features, or using weighted least squares.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0100","card":"q-ml-algorithms-022-5-7-assumptions","sec":"ml-algorithms-s021","q":"Does a violated assumption mean you must discard a linear model?","a":"<p>Not always. If it predicts well on held-out data and the business benefit is clear, a mildly violated assumption may be acceptable. Violations matter most when you rely on coefficient interpretation or confidence intervals.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0101","card":"q-ml-algorithms-023-5-8-challenge-predict-titanic-fare","sec":"ml-algorithms-s022","q":"A linear regression has a modest R² on training data. What does that suggest and what would you try?","a":"<p>The features either do not hold much information about the target or relate to it non-linearly. Add informative features (for example categorical ones after encoding), try a non-linear model, and check the residuals. Because training score is optimistic, the held-out score will be lower still.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0102","card":"q-ml-algorithms-023-5-8-challenge-predict-titanic-fare","sec":"ml-algorithms-s022","q":"Why drop the target column from X when predicting it, and what does this look like in code?","a":"<p>Leaving the target in X lets the model read the answer, giving a perfect but useless score. Build X by dropping the target column and keep it separately as y, for example <code>X = df.drop(columns=['fare'])</code> and <code>y = df['fare']</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0103","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"What is logistic regression and why is it called regression if it classifies?","a":"<p>It models the probability of a class by passing a linear combination of the features through the sigmoid function, which squashes any number into the range 0 to 1. The name comes from fitting a regression on the log-odds. Predicting class 1 when the probability is at least 0.5 turns it into a classifier.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0104","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"Why not use ordinary linear regression for a binary target?","a":"<p>A line produces values under 0 or over 1, which are not probabilities, and it is thrown off by points far from the boundary. The sigmoid keeps outputs in 0 to 1, and training with log loss suits probabilities.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0105","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"What loss does logistic regression minimize, and how does <code>LogisticRegression</code> regularize by default?","a":"<p>It minimizes log loss (cross-entropy), the negative log-likelihood of the labels. scikit-learn applies L2 regularization by default with strength controlled by <code>C</code>, where <code>C</code> is the inverse of regularization strength, so a smaller <code>C</code> means a stronger penalty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0106","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"How do you interpret a logistic regression coefficient?","a":"<p>It is the change in the log-odds of the positive class for a one-unit increase in the feature. <code>exp(coef)</code> is the odds ratio, so a coefficient of 0.7 multiplies the odds by about 2. A positive sign raises the probability of the positive class and a negative sign lowers it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0107","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"In scikit-learn, what is the difference between <code>predict</code> and <code>predict_proba</code> for a classifier?","a":"<p><code>predict</code> returns hard class labels, using a 0.5 threshold for binary logistic regression. <code>predict_proba</code> returns the probability of each class. Use probabilities when you need to rank, calibrate, or choose a different threshold for the business cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0108","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"How does logistic regression handle more than two classes?","a":"<p>Either one-vs-rest (a binary model per class) or multinomial (softmax) over all classes at once. With the default <code>lbfgs</code> solver, scikit-learn fits a multinomial model for more than two classes (since version 0.22 via <code>multi_class='auto'</code>). The <code>multi_class</code> parameter is deprecated since 1.5 and removed in 1.7; for one-vs-rest, wrap the estimator in <code>OneVsRestClassifier</code>. <code>coef_</code> then has one row per class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0109","card":"q-ml-algorithms-025-6-1-basic-1d-example","sec":"ml-algorithms-s024","q":"Where is the decision boundary of a one-feature logistic regression, and how do you compute it from the fitted model?","a":"<p>It is where the probability is exactly 0.5, which is where the linear term is zero. Solve <code>coef_[0][0] * x + intercept_[0] = 0</code> to get <code>x = -intercept / coef</code>. Points on one side are predicted 1 and on the other side 0.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0110","card":"q-ml-algorithms-025-6-1-basic-1d-example","sec":"ml-algorithms-s024","q":"A logistic regression predicts 0 for a customer whose probability is 0.45, but missing a positive case is costly. What do you do?","a":"<p>Lower the decision threshold, for example classify as positive at 0.3, using <code>predict_proba</code> and comparing against your threshold. This trades more false positives for fewer missed positives. Pick the threshold from the precision-recall curve and the relative costs of the two errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0111","card":"q-ml-algorithms-025-6-1-basic-1d-example","sec":"ml-algorithms-s024","q":"Does logistic regression need perfect separation of the two classes to work?","a":"<p>No. It tolerates overlap and some misclassified points, and outputs a probability. Perfectly separable data is actually a problem for unregularized fits, because the weights grow without bound. The default L2 penalty in scikit-learn keeps them finite.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0112","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"Why does a classifier that scores 99.9% accuracy on fraud detection still possibly fail?","a":"<p>If only 0.1 percent of transactions are fraud, always predicting \"not fraud\" gets 99.9 percent accuracy and finds no fraud. With imbalanced classes use precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC or ROC-AUC, class weights (<code>class_weight='balanced'</code>), or resampling, and tie the metric to business cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0113","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"Why scale the features before fitting a <code>LogisticRegression</code> in scikit-learn?","a":"<p>The default L2 penalty depends on feature scale, so unscaled features are penalized unevenly. The <code>lbfgs</code> solver also converges faster on scaled data, and unscaled data often triggers a <code>ConvergenceWarning</code>. Scaling also makes coefficients comparable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0114","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"For a multiclass <code>LogisticRegression</code>, what shape does <code>coef_</code> have and how do you chart one class's weights?","a":"<p>Shape is <code>(n_classes, n_features)</code>: one row of weights per class. Take a row such as <code>coef_[0]</code>, build <code>pd.Series(row, index=X.columns)</code> and plot it. Positive weights push toward that class and negative weights away from it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0115","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"Why split data with <code>train_test_split</code> before evaluating a model?","a":"<p>A model can memorize training rows, so its score on them is optimistic. Scoring on rows it never saw estimates how it will do on new data. The default hold-out is 25 percent, and <code>random_state</code> makes the split repeatable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0116","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"For classification with imbalanced labels, what does the <code>stratify</code> argument of <code>train_test_split</code> do?","a":"<p><code>stratify=y</code> keeps the class proportions the same in the train and test parts. Without it, a small split can end up with a very different class mix, which makes scores noisy or misleading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0117","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"Why should the <code>StandardScaler</code> be fit on the training split only?","a":"<p>Fitting on all data lets test-set statistics (mean and standard deviation) influence the training pipeline, a form of data leakage that makes test scores slightly optimistic. Split first, call <code>fit_transform</code> on train, and <code>transform</code> on test. A <code>Pipeline</code> does this automatically inside cross-validation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0118","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"Why might <code>XGBClassifier</code> score no better than <code>LogisticRegression</code> on a small tabular problem?","a":"<p>With few, mostly numeric features and little data, a linear boundary can be close to the best available and boosted trees have little extra signal to find, while they add variance. No model wins everywhere, so compare on held-out data and pick the simplest that performs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0119","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"How does a decision tree decide where to split?","a":"<p>It tries candidate features and thresholds and keeps the split that most reduces impurity in the child nodes: Gini or entropy for classification, squared error for regression. It repeats this recursively until a stopping rule such as <code>max_depth</code>, or pure leaves, is met.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0120","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"Why do decision trees need no feature scaling?","a":"<p>Each split looks at one feature and asks whether it is under a threshold. Rescaling a feature only moves the threshold and leaves the ordering unchanged, so the tree is identical. This contrasts with distance-based models and regularized linear models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0121","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of a single decision tree?","a":"<p>Advantages are that it needs little preprocessing, is easy to explain and visualize, and captures non-linear patterns and interactions. Disadvantages are that it overfits easily, is unstable (small data changes give a different tree), makes only axis-aligned splits, and predicts piecewise-constant values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0122","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"Can a decision tree extrapolate beyond the range of its training target?","a":"<p>No. A regression tree predicts the mean of the training targets in a leaf, so predictions stay within the range seen in training. For trending data a linear model, or a tree model on de-trended data, extrapolates better.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0123","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"How do tree models handle missing values and categorical columns?","a":"<p>XGBoost handles missing values natively by learning a default direction at each split, and supports categorical columns when <code>enable_categorical=True</code>. scikit-learn trees traditionally required imputing and encoding first. Newer releases added missing-value support to some tree estimators, so check the docs for your version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0124","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"What is the difference between Gini impurity and entropy as split criteria?","a":"<p>Both measure how mixed the classes are in a node. Gini is <code>1 - sum(p^2)</code> and entropy is <code>-sum(p log p)</code>. They usually give very similar trees, and Gini is slightly cheaper because it avoids the logarithm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0125","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"What is a decision stump and what does a regression stump predict?","a":"<p>A stump is a tree with depth 1, a single split. For regression it predicts the mean target of the training rows on each side of the threshold, so its output has two flat levels.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0126","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"How does <code>max_depth</code> relate to underfitting and overfitting in a decision tree?","a":"<p>A very shallow tree is too simple and underfits. With <code>max_depth=None</code> the tree grows until leaves are pure and memorizes the training data, so it overfits. A moderate depth balances the two. <code>max_depth</code> is a hyperparameter you set before training and tune by validation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0127","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"What other hyperparameters limit a scikit-learn decision tree besides <code>max_depth</code>?","a":"<p><code>min_samples_leaf</code> (minimum rows in a leaf), <code>min_samples_split</code>, <code>max_leaf_nodes</code>, and <code>ccp_alpha</code> for cost-complexity pruning after growth. They all restrict complexity and reduce variance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0128","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"Why is <code>x1.reshape(-1, 1)</code> needed in this prediction call on a fitted scikit-learn <code>DecisionTreeRegressor</code> with one feature?","a":"<p><code>np.linspace</code> returns a 1D array of 100 values, while <code>predict</code> needs a 2D array of shape (n_samples, n_features). <code>reshape(-1, 1)</code> turns it into 100 rows by 1 column. The plotted line is a staircase because the tree predicts a constant per leaf.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x1 = np.linspace(4, 14, 100)\ny1 = dt.predict(x1.reshape(-1, 1))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0129","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"In scikit-learn, what does <code>sklearn.tree.plot_tree</code> show and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It draws a fitted tree: each node shows the split rule, impurity, sample count and predicted value, with colors for the prediction. It lets you trace how any row is routed to its prediction, which is the interpretability benefit of trees.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0130","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"Why is a depth sweep scored only on the training data misleading?","a":"<p>Training score keeps rising as depth grows because a deep tree can memorize the data, so it always points to the deepest tree. You need held-out data to see where generalization stops improving.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0131","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"How do you read a validation curve that plots train and test scores against <code>max_depth</code>?","a":"<p>Train score climbs toward its maximum as complexity grows. Test score rises, peaks, then falls once the model starts to memorize noise. Left of the peak is underfitting, right of it is overfitting. Choose the depth near the peak, or the shallower one if scores are close.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0132","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"How would you build a validation curve for a tree-depth hyperparameter?","a":"<p>Split the data once, loop over depth values, fit a <code>DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=i)</code> on the training part, record <code>score</code> on train and on test, then plot both against depth. <code>sklearn.model_selection.validation_curve</code> does this with cross-validation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0133","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"A single decision tree scores lower than linear regression on a tabular regression task. How can that be?","a":"<p>If the true relationship is mostly smooth and additive, a line captures it with few parameters, while a single tree approximates it with steps and overfits noise. Trees pull ahead when there are strong non-linearities or interactions, and ensembles of trees pull ahead further.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0134","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What is bagging and how does a random forest apply it?","a":"<p>Bagging trains many models independently on bootstrap samples (rows drawn with replacement) and averages their predictions (regression) or takes a vote (classification). A random forest also considers only a random subset of features at each split, which decorrelates the trees. Averaging many noisy but different trees cuts variance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0135","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What is boosting and how does XGBoost apply it?","a":"<p>Boosting builds trees one after another, each fit to the errors (gradient of the loss) of the ensemble so far, and sums their scaled outputs. XGBoost adds regularization, shrinkage through <code>learning_rate</code>, column and row subsampling, and fast histogram-based training. It mainly reduces bias.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0136","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do bagging and boosting differ in training, parallelism and overfitting?","a":"<p>Bagging trains independent trees, so it parallelizes fully, and mostly reduces variance, which makes it hard to overfit by adding trees. Boosting is sequential because each tree depends on the last, though XGBoost parallelizes inside each tree, and it can overfit if you add too many trees, so it needs <code>learning_rate</code>, depth and early stopping tuned.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0137","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What do <code>n_estimators</code> and <code>max_depth</code> control in a scikit-learn <code>RandomForestRegressor</code>?","a":"<p><code>n_estimators</code> is the number of trees. More trees reduce variance and plateau, at a cost in time and memory. <code>max_depth</code> limits each tree's depth, and the default of <code>None</code> grows full trees, which is fine for forests because averaging controls overfitting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0138","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What is out-of-bag (OOB) scoring in a random forest?","a":"<p>Each tree is trained on a bootstrap sample that leaves out about a third of rows. Those left-out rows can score that tree, and averaging over all trees gives a validation estimate without a separate hold-out set. Enable it with <code>oob_score=True</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0139","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"Which XGBoost hyperparameters do you tune first?","a":"<p><code>learning_rate</code> (shrinks each tree's contribution), <code>n_estimators</code> (number of trees), <code>max_depth</code>, <code>subsample</code> and <code>colsample_bytree</code> (row and column sampling), and regularization such as <code>reg_lambda</code>. A lower learning rate with more trees generalizes better but trains slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0140","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do you stop XGBoost from adding too many trees?","a":"<p>Use early stopping on a validation set: training halts when the validation metric has not improved for a number of rounds. In XGBoost 1.6 and later you set <code>early_stopping_rounds</code> in the estimator constructor and pass <code>eval_set</code> to <code>fit</code>. Version 2.0 removed the older <code>fit</code> argument.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0141","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"When would you choose a random forest over XGBoost, and the reverse?","a":"<p>A random forest works well with little tuning, is robust and hard to overfit, so it is a good quick strong baseline. XGBoost usually reaches higher accuracy on tabular data when tuned and handles missing values, but is more sensitive to hyperparameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0142","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"Why is a random forest better than a single decision tree?","a":"<p>A single deep tree has high variance: it fits noise and changes a lot with small data changes. Averaging many decorrelated trees keeps their low bias and cancels much of the variance, so test error is lower and more stable. The cost is lost interpretability and slower prediction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0143","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"What is wrong with picking a tree depth by looking at the test-set score?","a":"<p>You tune on the test data, so the final test score is no longer an unbiased estimate: it leaks. Tune with cross-validation or a separate validation set, and touch the test set once at the end.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0144","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"How does k-fold cross-validation work and why is it better than one train/test split?","a":"<p>Split the training data into k folds (often 5), train on k-1 and score on the held-out fold, rotate through all folds, and average the scores. Every row is used for validation once, and the spread across folds shows how stable the estimate is. One split can be lucky or unlucky.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0145","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"In scikit-learn, how would you pick the best <code>max_depth</code> automatically with cross-validation?","a":"<p><code>GridSearchCV</code> fits the tree for every depth in the grid with 5-fold cross-validation, and <code>best_params_</code> holds the depth with the best mean validation score. It then refits the best model on all training data, available as <code>best_estimator_</code>. Score the final model once on the test set.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nsearch = GridSearchCV(DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=0),\n                      param_grid={&#x27;max_depth&#x27;: range(1, 11)}, cv=5)\nsearch.fit(X_train, y_train)\nsearch.best_params_</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0146","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"For classification, why use stratified k-fold?","a":"<p>It keeps the class proportions in every fold. With imbalanced classes a plain split can put almost no positives in a fold. scikit-learn uses stratified folds by default when you pass an integer <code>cv</code> to a classifier.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0147","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"Why prefer the shallower depth when several depths give similar validation scores?","a":"<p>Simpler models are cheaper, easier to explain, and less likely to be overfit to noise, so the flat part of the curve at lower depth is the safer choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0148","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"In scikit-learn, what do <code>predict</code> and <code>transform</code> return for <code>KMeans</code> versus <code>PCA</code>?","a":"<p>For <code>KMeans</code>, <code>predict(X)</code> returns the cluster index for each row, and <code>transform(X)</code> returns distances to each centroid. For <code>PCA</code>, <code>transform(X)</code> returns the principal component scores, and there is no <code>predict</code>. Learned attributes are <code>cluster_centers_</code> for K-means and <code>components_</code> and <code>explained_variance_ratio_</code> for PCA.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0149","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"How would you approach a new tabular prediction problem, from baseline to final model?","a":"<p>Look at and plot the data, split off a test set, build a simple baseline (linear or logistic regression, or a single tree), then try a random forest and XGBoost. Tune with cross-validation, compare on held-out data, pick the simplest model that meets the business need, and score on the test set once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0150","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"Which algorithms need feature scaling and which do not?","a":"<p>Scaling matters for distance-based and gradient or penalty-based methods: K-means, PCA, regularized linear and logistic regression, and neural networks. Tree-based models (decision tree, random forest, XGBoost) are unaffected.</p>","hand":true}];
